("The history of the bicycle","Section 4",
 "Today I'd like to talk about the early history of the bicycle. The first machine of this kind, built in the early nineteenth century, had no pedals at all. Riders pushed along with their feet, so it was more like a scooter with a seat. Pedals appeared on the front wheel in the eighteen sixties, and these machines were called boneshakers because of their wooden wheels. Later, the front wheel was made very large to increase speed, but this design was dangerous, as riders fell from a great height. The breakthrough came with the safety bicycle in the eighteen eighties, which had two wheels of equal size and a chain driving the back wheel. Rubber tyres filled with air soon made riding far more comfortable. Within a decade, bicycles had become popular with ordinary people, and many historians argue that they helped women gain greater independence.",
 [("What was unusual about the first machine?",["It had three wheels","It had no pedals","It was made of metal","It had a motor"],1),
  ("Why were the pedal machines called boneshakers?",["They were very fast","They had wooden wheels","They were heavy","They had no seat"],1),
  ("What was the problem with the huge front wheel?",["It was slow","It was dangerous","It was expensive","It was ugly"],1),
  ("What made the safety bicycle different?",["Two wheels of equal size and a chain","A larger front wheel","A wooden frame","Three gears"],0),
  ("What do many historians say about bicycles?",["They replaced trains","They helped women gain independence","They were unpopular","They were banned"],1)]),
("Why we dream","Section 4",
 "Scientists have long debated why we dream. One theory says that dreams help the brain sort the day's memories, strengthening the important ones and discarding the rest. A second theory claims that dreaming is a kind of rehearsal for danger, which would explain why so many dreams involve being chased. A third idea is more modest: dreams may simply be a by-product of brain activity during sleep, with no purpose at all. Evidence for the first theory comes from experiments in which people who learned a new task and then slept performed better the next day. However, these results do not prove that dreams were responsible, since deep sleep may be the real cause. Researchers agree on one point: people who are deprived of the dreaming stage of sleep tend to become irritable and forgetful.",
 [("What does the first theory say dreams do?",["Predict the future","Help sort memories","Cause tiredness","Prepare us for work"],1),
  ("What does the second theory explain?",["Why dreams are colourful","Why many dreams involve being chased","Why we sleep","Why dreams are forgotten"],1),
  ("What does the third idea suggest?",["Dreams have an important purpose","Dreams are a by-product with no purpose","Dreams are caused by food","Dreams only happen in children"],1),
  ("Why do the experiments not prove the first theory?",["Too few people","Deep sleep might be the real cause","They were not published","They were too short"],1),
  ("What happens to people deprived of dreaming sleep?",["They become irritable and forgetful","They sleep longer","They dream more later","They become healthier"],0)]),
("Lecture: coral reefs","Section 4",
 "Coral reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, yet they support about a quarter of all marine species. This is why scientists often call them the rainforests of the sea. Corals are tiny animals that live in partnership with algae. The algae give the coral its colour and supply food through photosynthesis. When the water becomes too warm, the coral expels the algae, turns white and may die. This process, called bleaching, has become more common in recent decades. Other threats include pollution from farms, which encourages harmful algae to grow, and damage from boat anchors. On a positive note, some reefs have recovered when fishing was restricted and water quality improved. Scientists are also growing corals in nurseries and transplanting them to damaged areas, although this is expensive and cannot yet be done on a large scale.",
 [("What proportion of the ocean floor do reefs cover?",["More than a quarter","About half","Less than one per cent","Exactly ten per cent"],2),
  ("What do the algae provide for the coral?",["Protection from fish","Colour and food","Warmth","Shelter from storms"],1),
  ("What causes bleaching?",["Cold water","Water that is too warm","Strong currents","Lack of light"],1),
  ("How does farm pollution harm reefs?",["It warms the water","It encourages harmful algae","It kills fish directly","It blocks sunlight completely"],1),
  ("Why is transplanting coral limited?",["It is illegal","It is expensive and cannot be done at scale","It never works","There is no coral to use"],1)]),
